Source: world-keyboard-windows
Section: x11
Priority: optional
Maintainer: @MAINTAINER@
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: world-keyboard-windows
Architecture: all
Depends: xkb-data, ${misc:Depends}
Description: extra keyboard layouts that type like Windows (prototype)
 PROTOTYPE, not released. Adds new keyboard layouts that type, key for key,
 what the same layout types on Windows, next to the Linux layouts, which
 stay as they are. Each new layout is the Linux layout with only the keys
 changed where Windows differs (Caps Lock behaviour, a few AltGr
 characters), generated from Unicode CLDR 43 Windows keyboard data and
 checked by machine. Not covered: Ctrl+Alt as AltGr.
 .
 Layouts in this prototype (151), each named "(as on Windows)": European
 layouts in Latin and Cyrillic script, and layouts in Arabic, Indic,
 Southeast Asian, Caucasian, Mongolian, African and historic scripts. They
 show up in the desktop's layout list. Where Windows and the Linux layout
 differ, the new layout follows Windows; the Linux layout stays as it is.
 .
 Optional, per user: "world-keyboard-windows accent-space on" makes the
 accent keys give what Windows gives (for example an accent followed by
 Space gives the accent itself; Linux gives an apostrophe or a quote). It
 writes a small ~/.XCompose and never changes one of the user's own.
 .
 It does not touch the belgian-azerty-windows package, the rules or any
 setting. To make the layouts selectable in Cinnamon (Linux Mint), which
 reads only xkb-data's layout list, it diverts that list (rules/evdev.xml)
 and rebuilds it as xkb-data's file plus its own entries; removal puts
 xkb-data's file back. Installing it only makes the layouts available.
